        <p class="article__lead reveal">Rwanda sits just south of the equator, so it's a year round destination, gorilla permits are issued in every month. What changes with the seasons is how firm the forest trails are, where the game gathers in Akagera, and how green and quiet the country feels. Here's how to choose.</p>

        <h2 class="reveal">Rwanda's seasons at a glance</h2>
        <p class="reveal">There are two drier spells, <strong>June to September</strong> (the long dry season) and <strong>December to February</strong> (a shorter dry window), and two wetter ones, the long rains (<strong>March to May</strong>) and the short rains (<strong>October to November</strong>). As an upland, equatorial country, Rwanda is mild and green all year; even in the dry months a forest shower is always possible.</p>

        <h2 class="reveal">Best for gorilla &amp; chimp trekking</h2>
        <p class="reveal">The drier months, <strong>June to September</strong> and <strong>December to February</strong>, are ideal for trekking in the <a href="volcanoes-national-park.html">Volcanoes National Park</a> and <a href="nyungwe-forest.html">Nyungwe</a>, when the steep trails are firmest. Rwanda's gorilla permits are premium priced and strictly limited, so we book yours well ahead, especially for these peak windows. Trekking has a minimum age of 15.</p>

        <h2 class="reveal">Best for safari</h2>
        <p class="reveal">For the savanna of <a href="akagera.html">Akagera</a>, the dry months concentrate wildlife around the lakes and keep the tracks easy, with June to September especially reliable for big game. The green seasons are lush, scenic and excellent for the park's superb birdlife.</p>

        <h2 class="reveal">The green seasons</h2>
        <p class="reveal">The wetter months (March to May and October to November) bring the deepest greens, dramatic skies, fewer trekkers and often lower lodge rates. Gorilla permits remain available, so flexible travellers who don't mind a muddier hike can enjoy the forest in beautiful solitude.</p>

        <h2 class="reveal">When to think twice</h2>
        <p class="reveal">The heart of the long rains (<strong>April to May</strong>) can make the forest trails genuinely demanding. For first time visitors set on a comfortable trek we usually steer toward the dry windows, but with good boots and the right spirit, the green season can be wonderfully atmospheric and rewarding.</p>

